    "result_text": "The full suite is green: **55 tests run, 0 failures, 0 errors**.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented customerCode de-duplication in `AssignCustomerCode.java` (the step in the `POST /api/owners` OfficeFloor pipeline that assigns the code).\n\n**The change:** After computing the base code `<REGION>-<HASH8>`, the step now checks it against the `customerCode` of every existing owner. On a collision it appends `-<n>`, trying `n = 2, 3, 4, \u2026` and stopping at the smallest value that produces a unique code; that de-duplicated code is what gets stored on the owner.\n\nKey details that made this correct and safe:\n- The step runs **before** `SaveOwner`, so the new owner isn't yet in the repository and is never compared against itself \u2014 matching the existing pattern in sibling steps like `AssignNamesakeCount` and `EnsureUniqueIdentity`.\n- It uses the injected `OwnerRepository` (added as a second `@Val`-free bean parameter, injected by type per the OfficeFloor convention) and collects existing codes into a `HashSet` for lookup.\n- The loop bottoms out immediately (`n` never used) in the common no-collision case, preserving the plain `<REGION>-<HASH8>` format that tests like Cp09/Cp16/Cp32 assert.\n\nThe relevant acceptance test `Cp41Tests` (distinct owners always get distinct customerCodes) passes, along with all other customerCode-dependent tests (Cp09, Cp14, Cp16, Cp21, Cp31, Cp32) and the full suite.",
